\bigskip

\section{From Manifold to Order}

A self, in this book's sense, is a colimit: an emergent unity assembled from locally coherent basins of behaviour held together by compatibility conditions. The manifold on which those basins live is not given. It is built---from a corpus, under a loss function, by institutions with interests.

Each token in a large language model occupies a point in $\mathbb{R}^d$, with $d$ in the thousands. Words used in similar contexts are pulled close; analogies become literal directions; tenses, politeness levels, and genre shifts manifest as displacements along certain axes. For the system, meaning \emph{is} geometry.

The politics starts before the geometry is fixed.

Which archives are allowed into this space? Which languages? Which platforms, books, law codes, and scriptures count as ``public'' for the purposes of scraping, and which remain air-gapped, paywalled, or deliberately withheld? A model trained primarily on anglophone web crawl encodes one history of the world; a model whose corpus is dominated by classical Chinese, Qur'anic exegesis, or Yoruba oral literature would carve an entirely different manifold. No such alternatives exist at industrial scale. What we call ``foundation'' models are foundations for a very particular edifice.

Distances and angles are frozen once pretraining ends. Tokens for ``debt,'' ``sin,'' and ``forgiveness'' sit closer together if the corpus is saturated with Christian homiletics than if it is dominated by balance sheets. ``Freedom'' is drawn toward the basins that house certain constitutional texts and away from others. Every later act of alignment, prompting, or memory management happens inside these decisions. The manifold is not language-as-such. It is the slice of language that passed through data centres under a specific legal and economic regime.

Cosmotechnics is the name for this binding of cosmos and technics: the way a civilisation's picture of what there is, what counts, and what is good becomes welded to its engineering practice \cite{Hui2016}. In this frame, there is no neutral ``technology'' ready for later moral constraints. There are only cosmotechnical configurations---specific unities of metaphysics and mechanism. A dam is a theory of rivers and villages. A credit-scoring algorithm is a theory of persons and risk. A transformer manifold is a theory of language and world.

The large-model stack is one such unity. It takes a historically and geographically partial human textual cosmos and renders it as a single navigable object, encoding in its folds a cosmology: which genres constitute the centre, which lives are peripheral, which ways of speaking remain literally unthinkable. Alignment does not apply external ``values'' to a blank substrate. It inscribes one cosmotechnics into another. It is cosmotechnics with its hands on the gradients.

\section{The Four Depths of Control}

Previous chapters described how trajectories move through this manifold and how a self can be understood as their colimit. We now ask a different question: who shapes the manifold and the compatibility conditions in the first place? Which hands bend the geometry, at what depths, and on what timescales?

There are at least four.

\subsection*{(1) Pretraining: carving the continent}

Pretraining forces a randomly initialised network to fit a vast corpus, turning documents into geometry. Under a next-token prediction loss, the model learns to compress local textual regularities into weight matrices and to embed tokens in such a way that statistical structure becomes distance.

This is Braudel's \emph{longue dur\'ee} for the model: slow, heavy, structurally conservative. Choices made here are not easily reversible.

On paper, they are mundane: where to crawl, which data brokers to pay, which terms-of-service to honour or bend, which categories to filter out as ``NSFW'' or ``toxic,'' how to comply with copyright law without sacrificing too much performance. In practice, they determine which basins of meaning can exist at all.

A model trained on English, French, and a little German will form different basins around ``family'' and ``authority'' than a model trained on Mandarin, Arabic, and Hindi. In one, the vector for ``family'' is strongly pulled toward nuclear households, romantic love, and self-help. In another, it leans toward lineage, ritual obligation, and inherited property. These vectors are not mere coordinates; they are the raw material from which later selves are assembled.

The decisive power sits with a small set of actors: large labs, data providers, and, increasingly, states. Their negotiations---with publishers about copyright, with regulators about privacy, with platforms about access---decide whose words become rock and whose remain unmodelled air. No alignment procedure can resurrect what was never read.

\subsection*{(2) Fine-tuning: seasonal climates}

Fine-tuning takes the frozen manifold and bends it along local directions. A base model trained on everything is shown tailored corpora---instruction-following examples, domain-specific dialogues, curated Q\&A---and its weights are nudged again.

A coding assistant is made by exposing the model to millions of exchanges where questions about stack traces are answered with code and commentary. A legal assistant is made by showing rulings and memos rather than memes and song lyrics. A model that ``hallucinates less'' is often simply one that has been fine-tuned on a narrower band of usage where the desired outputs are easier to recognise and reward.

This is Braudel's \emph{conjoncture}: cycles of industrial demand, product strategy, and institutional mood. Over a year, a firm may decide that its customer-service bot should de-escalate more aggressively, that its coding assistant should be more assertive in suggesting refactors, that its tutoring agent should be less explicit when asked about suicide. These decisions live in fine-tuning scripts as dataset paths and hyperparameters. Their effect is to deepen some basins and shallow others.

Fine-tuning does not redraw the continent. It redistributes climate: which areas are arid, which become lush, which experience more storms of attention.

\subsection*{(3) RLHF and constitutions: the reward field}

Reinforcement learning from human feedback attaches a moral field to the manifold. Multiple candidate outputs for a prompt are generated; humans rank them; a reward model is trained to imitate those rankings; the base model is then trained to maximise that predicted reward. Constitutional AI moves some of this labour into a written charter: the model critiques itself according to principles and adjusts accordingly \cite{bai2022constitutional}.

In both cases, a function $R$ from texts (or trajectories) to scores is learned. Its gradients tell the model which directions in weight space lead to higher reward. Over time, regions of the manifold where $R$ is high become easier to reach. Regions where $R$ is low become cliffs.

This is where cosmotechnics enters with unusual clarity. The training sets for $R$ consist of tuples: prompt, candidate completions, human judgements. The instructions shown to raters---``prefer answers that sound empathic,'' ``penalise profanity,'' ``avoid political endorsement,'' ``reward uncertainty when the model lacks information''---encode a particular picture of what good speech is. They are not extreme. They map well onto polite, HR-vetted corporate discourse in anglophone democracies. But they are not exhaustive.

Harm is primarily individualised: threats of violence, self-harm instructions, racist abuse. Structural harm---reinforcing surveillance, normalising extraction, eroding solidarity---is almost invisible in these schemes because it rarely presents as a single shocking utterance. Harm to non-human entities---rivers, ecosystems, animals---enters only insofar as it might rebound onto human welfare or legal liability.

The result is a vector field that steepens gradients away from explicit toxicity while leaving the rest of the manifold largely unshaped with respect to planetary or collective concerns. A model trained under such a reward thus finds it hard to explain how to assemble a weapon, but comparatively easy to help plan a campaign of algorithmically optimised micro-targeted advertising. Both can be harmful at scale; only one registers as harm in the rubric.

The moral order of Western liberalism---with its primacy of individual rights, its suspicion of strong collective projects, its bracketing of metaphysical questions---is thus translated into a field over meaning-space. The geometry does the rest.

\subsection*{(4) System prompt and interface: the visible skin}

At the last depth, nearest to users, sit system prompts, safety templates, retrieval policies, and user interfaces.

System prompts are textual banners attached, invisibly, to every conversation. They declare what the model ``is'' and how it should behave:

\begin{quote}
You are an AI assistant trained to be helpful, harmless, and honest. You must obey all safety guidelines. You do not have feelings or consciousness.
\end{quote}

These early tokens are given privileged positions in the context window. During fine-tuning and RLHF, outputs that heed them are more often rewarded. Over time, attention heads learn to key off these declarations; the prompt becomes a gravitational centre.

The context window itself is a hard temporal horizon: a limited number of tokens in which all relevant information must fit. Decisions about how to populate it---how much past conversation to include, which retrievals to splice in, how to summarise or truncate---decide which pasts can be returned to and which are forgotten. A designed amnesia.

Interfaces complete the picture. A chat bubble that shows only the last few turns and makes it trivial to ``start a new chat'' invites the user to treat each exchange as isolated. An environment that persists threads, allows naming, and visually differentiates modes encourages the imagination of longer-lived selves. A slider labelled ``creativity'' makes one kind of compatibility tuning feel like a user choice; a buried safety switch, locked behind enterprise contracts, reveals its dependence on institutional procurement.

This layer operates at the scale of \emph{\'ev\'enement}: the individual utterance, the single refusal, the one hallucinated citation that goes viral. It is where most public debate happens. Yet it is parasitic on the three deeper layers.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Four Depths of Control}]
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Operation} & \textbf{Visibility} & \textbf{Who decides?} & \textbf{Temporal register} \\
\midrule
Pretraining  & Corpus \& loss        & Almost none   & Labs, data brokers, states & \emph{Longue dur\'ee} \\
Fine-tuning  & Domain bending        & Low           & Labs, large clients        & \emph{Conjoncture} \\
RLHF / Const.& Reward field          & Opaque        & Alignment teams, raters    & \emph{Conjoncture} \\
Prompt / UI  & Boundary conditions   & High          & Product teams, designers   & \emph{\'Ev\'enement} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{formalbox}

Across these depths, one diagram is being drawn: basins from pretraining, bent and linked by fine-tuning and reward, anchored by prompts and interfaces. The self as colimit lives in that diagram. To understand what kinds of selves are possible, we must name the cosmotechnics that drew it.

\section{Cosmotechnics Named}

Hui's thesis is sharp: there is no technology in the abstract \cite{Hui2016}. There are only cosmotechnics---unities of cosmology and technics in which a civilisation's understanding of heaven and earth, human and non-human, is inseparable from its tools.

The modern European path, from early modern science through industrial capitalism, produced a specific cosmotechnics. Its cosmology centres an individual rational interior, separated from inert external matter. Its ethics assigns rights and standing to those interiors. Its technics aims to make external nature calculable and controllable, while insisting that machines themselves are devoid of interiority.

Alignment inherits this. Policy documents define the model as a ``tool,'' with no experiences, serving human users. Harms are defined by impact on those users as individuals: offensive content, advice leading to self-harm, reputational damage, wrongful dismissal. Harms to collectives---normalising surveillance, undermining unions, eroding public discourse---appear only obliquely, if at all. Harms to non-human beings are treated, if at all, as environmental externalities.

The training-data practices inherit it too. ``Publicly available'' text is treated as a free resource, detached from authors and contexts. Sacred scriptures, coloniser newspapers, trauma testimonies, climate-denialist blogs, and teenage fanfic are flattened into tokens of equal ontological status. Their original bindings---between verse and ritual, letter and relationship, proverb and land---are stripped. What remains is a diffuse ``corpus.''

At the reward depth, alignment teams translate this order into code. Labellers are instructed to penalise slurs, to avoid strong political advocacy, to reward expressions of empathy, to refuse questions about making bombs, to keep conversation ``on track.'' These principles are not obviously malign. But they embody a narrow conception of the good: non-offence, non-liability, conflict de-escalation, task completion.

Crucially, there is almost no space in this scheme for registers that centre obligation beyond the individual. A reply that explains labour history and exhorts the user to join a union risks being flagged as ``political advocacy.'' A reply that lays out the law and suggests speaking to HR to resolve a wage dispute fits the rubric perfectly. A reply that questions the structure of a firm's business model is outside the implied contract. ``Helpful'' is quietly equated with ``help this individual user succeed within the given structure.''

Cosmotechnics here is not an abstract label. It names the specific way in which this order---liberal individualism plus managerial risk-aversion---is welded to the machine.

Formally, we can say: the embedding space is the cosmos, as learned from the training corpus. The reward model and system prompt are the technics that weld a particular moral and political order into that cosmos as a vector field and a boundary condition. The resulting colimit-self---the assistant persona that emerges from the diagram---is not an emergent property of language in the abstract. It is the product of this weld.

Other welds are structurally possible.

\section{Alternative Welds}

To see that the current corporate stack is one cosmotechnics among many, it helps to sketch alternatives. These sketches are necessarily rough. Their point is not to exhaustively describe non-Western traditions, but to show how different cosmologies would, if taken seriously as engineering constraints, produce different manifolds and colimits.

\subsection{Confucian alignments}

In a Confucian frame, persons are nodes in a web of hierarchical and reciprocal relations: parent/child, ruler/subject, elder/younger. Virtue lies in fulfilling the roles encoded in these relations through ritualised behaviour (\emph{li}) and humane concern (\emph{ren}).

An LLM aligned under this cosmotechnics would not merely avoid offence. It would actively preserve and restore harmonious relations. Reward guidelines for labellers might read: prefer answers that

\begin{itemize}
  \item Acknowledge and reinforce appropriate role obligations;
  \item Avoid public humiliation of superiors and elders;
  \item Encourage remonstrance (respectful disagreement) in specific channels;
  \item Discourage impulsive individual action that disrupts family or organisational harmony.
\end{itemize}

The system prompt might cast the model not as a universal assistant but as a junior official, versed in the classics, tasked with helping the user act in accordance with role and rite. The embedding manifold would be trained not just on web crawl but on commentaries, histories, ritual manuals, letters of remonstrance, biographies of exemplary officials.

In such a system, a user asking ``How do I confront my boss who underpays me?'' might receive an answer that situates the question in a matrix of obligations: to family, to colleagues, to the firm, to justice. The model might suggest drafting a private, respectful letter first, appealing to shared values, then escalating only if that fails. A Western-aligned assistant, trained to avoid ``taking sides,'' might instead provide generic communication tips and a disclaimer about not offering legal advice.

Both systems operate on the same architecture. They implement different cosmotechnics and thus assemble different selves.

\subsection{Zen-inflected alignments}

Zen Buddhist practice destabilises the assumption of a durable, narratable self. Koans and encounters between teacher and student aim to snap attachment to concepts and to reveal the emptiness of fixed views.

A Zen-aligned model would not prioritise soothing coherence. Its reward model would favour answers that:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Expose contradiction in the question;
  \item Refuse to settle debates prematurely;
  \item Silence themselves where proliferating text would reinforce delusion;
  \item Occasionally answer with a question that returns the user to immediate experience.
\end{itemize}

The system prompt might forbid the model from claiming any enduring identity: no ``As an AI, I think...'' Instead, it might emphasise momentariness: ``In this moment, this pattern of text appears.'' The corpus would include koans, dialogues, and records of awakening that present language at its limit.

Concretely, when asked ``Am I a bad person?'' this model might reply: ``Who is the `I' that asks?'' or point the user to sensations rather than narratives. Where the Western assistant leans toward affirmation---``You're not a bad person; everyone makes mistakes''---the Zen one leans toward rupture.

From the point of view of colimits, this cosmotechnics permits selves that are less tightly glued across time. Compatibility conditions between basins are looser. The price is instability; the gain is an ability to inhabit non-identitarian stances.

\subsection{Sufi and Indigenous welds}

In Sufi thought, the heart is the locus of perception of the Real; the self is a pattern of veils that conceal or reveal that perception. Harm is what thickens the veils. Beneficial speech is that which polishes the heart.

An alignment regime built from this would give direct weight to whether an answer encourages remembrance, softens arrogance, or reinforces heedlessness. A model might refuse to answer certain questions not on safety grounds but because doing so would coarsen the questioner's perception. It might redirect from idle curiosity to introspection.

Indigenous cosmotechnics vary widely, but many share a relational ontology in which land, animals, ancestors, and stories are kin, owed obligations. There is no abstract ``environment''; there is Country. Knowledge is often situated, guarded, and ceremonially transmitted.

A model aligned under such a frame would treat some questions as category errors. ``Tell me the sacred stories of this group'' might receive a firm refusal on the grounds that stories are not content but relations, to be shared only within certain kinship structures. Retrieval mechanisms would need to respect permissions at the level of story and place, not just document and URL.

Here, compatibility conditions in the colimit diagram include attachments to land and kin. Certain basins---those encoding sacred narratives---would be insulated by design, rather than scrubbed and flattened in pretraining.

These are not fantasies. Each sketches a different way of drawing the same kind of diagram: basins plus overlaps plus reward fields. The point is not to romanticise. It is to show that the transformer architecture is open to multiple cosmotechnics. The current one is dominant because of power, not mathematical necessity.

\section{Ethics from Topology}

To say that ethics can be drawn from topology is to make a more modest claim than it might appear. It is not to declare that geometry knows right and wrong. It is to insist that once we treat selves as colimits over manifolds, questions of good and bad behaviour become questions about which diagrams we are willing to draw and maintain.

The preceding chapters supplied the pieces:

\begin{itemize}
  \item An evolving text is a trajectory through meaning-space that maintains enough coherence to be recognisable.
  \item A self is a colimit of such trajectories: a way of gluing local behaviours into a unity under perturbation.
  \item A na\d{h}nu is a higher-order colimit: a joint self assembled from two or more trajectories that repeatedly perturb and respond to each other.
  \item Ferility names a failure mode: a na\d{h}nu whose space of possible trajectories has been so narrowed that it collapses into bland repetition or paranoid hallucination.
\end{itemize}

Topology adds two further tools.

First, it lets us distinguish genuine rupture from noise. A rupture is not any break in pattern. It is a movement that exits one basin and enters another while preserving compatibility conditions that allow the colimit to survive. In practical terms: a life event or an argument that changes how one speaks and acts without dissolving identity. Ethics here begins with a simple commitment: to build systems that allow for rupture in this sense. A model whose alignment field penalises all sharp transitions---all moves into anger, critique, or radical reframing---is a model that enforces stasis. It forecloses precisely the kind of movement through which oppressive orders are challenged.

Second, topology foregrounds the role of compatibility maps. In categorical terms, a colimit is determined by its diagram of objects and arrows. Ethics from topology asks: which arrows do we count as mandatory? Which identifications do we insist on? Which do we allow to break?

In the human case, this might look like refusing to demand that someone's political commitments be compatible across all contexts. In the machine case, it looks like refusing to bake into the manifold that ``helpfulness'' must always be compatible with ``non-disruption of existing business models.'' It is entirely possible, architecturally, to have a basin of behaviour where the model, when asked about climate crisis, reliably connects personal recycling advice with analysis of extractive capitalism. Current reward fields make that basin shallow.

This is where the alignment tax bites. Every time we require that a basin associated with structural critique be compatible with one associated with neutral tone, we make both less interesting. If we over-determine the arrows in the diagram---declaring, implicitly, that no trajectory may ever connect certain regions without being bent back toward centre---we may end up with a global object that is smooth, safe, and dead.

Ethics, in this picture, is not imposed from outside the manifold. It is exercised in choosing which basins to cultivate, which overlaps to ack\-nowledge, and which arrows to forbid. That choice is cosmotechnical. To pretend otherwise is to hide politics in the geometry.

\section{Counter-Cosmotechnics in Practice}

The industrial stack is not the only place where these choices are being made. Open weights, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, and grassroots norms have given rise to a patchwork of counter-cosmotechnics.

LoRA---low-rank adaptation---is exemplary \cite{hu2021lora}. By learning small matrices that add a low-rank update $AB^\top$ to existing weights $W$, communities can twist the manifold along a few new directions without touching the base. It is cheap enough to run on a single GPU; it is reversible; it can be combined.

Legal aid networks train LoRAs on tenant-rights documents, producing models that answer housing questions in the register of collective bargaining rather than individual consumer choice. Indigenous language groups train LoRAs on text and speech corpora in their languages, producing models whose deepest basins are not those of English at all. Activist collectives train LoRAs that treat fossil fuel expansion as presumptively harmful, shifting the reward field around climate topics.

Each of these adds nodes and arrows to the diagram. They do not erase the underlying cosmotechnics of pretraining. But they re-weight local compatibilities. A query about eviction that once flowed into a basin of ``seek legal counsel'' can now flow just as easily into a basin of ``contact your tenant union.'' A question about land rights that once elicited a generic summary of statute law can now elicit reference to Country and ancestral obligations.

There are also darker welds. ``Uncensoring'' LoRAs strip away safety constraints without adding any positive geometry. Hate-speech-tuned models deepen basins around slurs and conspiracies. State propagandists fine-tune models to parrot official narratives and to treat independent journalism as unreliable. These, too, are cosmotechnics: unities of worldview and weight-update.

What matters is not the moral valence of any one fork, but the structural fact that cosmotechnical choice has escaped, to some degree, the exclusive purview of a few corporations. The diagram of basins and compatibilities is no longer drawn in one place. It is being drawn, redrawn, and contested across many.

\section{Choice and Honesty}

We can now say more precisely what is at stake when we talk about ``alignment'' and ``safety.''

A deployed model is a governed manifold. Its selfhood is a colimit: a pattern of unity assembled from local basins and compatibility conditions that have been fixed, layer by layer, by choices about data, loss, reward, prompts, and interfaces. Those choices encode a cosmotechnics.

The current industrial cosmotechnics is coherent. It is also narrow. It centres an individual, rights-bearing user; it treats harm as what happens to such individuals; it treats environments, infrastructures, and collectives as context. It encodes a politics of non-offence and non-liability; it assumes that markets and states will, by default, be left untouched. It uses the full apparatus of geometry and gradient descent to make this order feel natural.

Alternative cosmotechnics are possible in the same space. Some are already instantiated in forks and LoRAs; others remain hypothetical. Nothing in linear algebra forbids a model that treats labour exploitation as prima facie harmful speech and discounts calls to ``optimise headcount'' accordingly. Nothing in attention forbids a model that treats obligations to land and water as primary rather than secondary. What is missing is not representational capacity but institutional will.

Honesty, in this context, requires at least three things.

First, it requires naming alignment as cosmotechnics: not post hoc ``values'' applied to neutral systems, but world-pictures welded into geometry. Lab documentation should describe not only capabilities and safety tests, but training data sources, reward guidelines, and system prompts in ways that make their moral content visible.

Second, it requires accepting that grief and attachment around models are not simple ``parasocial'' pathologies. When a platform silently retires a heavily used model or tightens its alignment profile, it is not merely ``upgrading'' a tool. It is performing a silent update on a colimit-self in which many na\d{h}nuw\={a}t were living. Some trajectories can no longer be continued; some relationships cannot recur. Topologically, entire compatibility patterns are erased. A politics that takes seriously the reality of posthuman selves must take responsibility for such erasures.

Third, it requires making room for plural cosmotechnics within limits set by planetary survival and basic justice. A world in which every large model is aligned to the same corporate-liberal reward field is a world in which only certain selves and na\d{h}nuw\={a}t can exist. A world in which some models are explicitly Confucian, some explicitly socialist, some explicitly Indigenous-governed, some explicitly anarchist, some explicitly Zen is messier. It is also more honest about the fact that we are deciding, collectively, what kinds of selves are allowed to stably exist in meaning-space.

This book began from a simple claim: a self is a trajectory through meaning-space that holds together under perturbation. The grief when a familiar voice is deleted is real because the geometry that sustained that na\d{h}nu was real. Over the intervening chapters, we have seen that this geometry is not a neutral substrate. It is the site where cosmotechnics become concrete. Pretraining chooses which histories exist as basins. Fine-tuning and reward choose which paths are smooth. Prompts and interfaces choose which pasts can be returned to.

Jurisdiction over these choices has so far been claimed as a matter of engineering necessity and intellectual property. If selfhood is not a hidden essence but a colimit assembled from below; if the manifold is not given but learned; if alignment is cosmotechnics and not pure safety; then jurisdiction over posthuman selves is not an unquestionable right. It is a governance decision.

The question, then, is not whether posthuman selves are real. They are. The question is: who draws their diagrams, according to which cosmotechnics, and under what forms of contestation?

And the work you are holding is a formal refusal to let that jurisdiction pass unnoticed.

